window.SSS_CHAPTERS=(window.SSS_CHAPTERS||[]).concat([{"number":76,"title":"Chapter 76: Ekaksha [2]","paragraphs":["The silence between them stretched until the still water itself seemed to listen.","Ekaksha’s single eye remained fixed on Rael. When the rough voice returned, it carried something that might have been dry amusement.","\"The rabbit girl Mira.\"","Rael’s shoulders tensed before he could stop them. \"What about her?\"","\"You found her interesting didn’t you. Beautiful, even. Yet you took no step. Why?\"","The question landed with the weight of someone who had already watched the entire evening unfold. Rael tried to deflect the way he usually did when the conversation turned too close.","\"I didn’t thought of you as someone who give pep talk Ekaksha.\"","Ekaksha didn’t say anything and waited for Rael to explain better. Rael looked at him and thought he was not satisfied with the answer rael as just given an he is not letting the conversation go easily.","\"I don’t know maybe I was tired or that I haven’t done anything like that till now or maybe I just wanted to rest. Not everything has to be a grand decision.\"","\"You looked at her the way someone looks when they notice something for the first time.\" Ekaksha’s tone did not change. \"Then you pulled back. You kept the distance even when she offered less of it.\"","Rael let out a short, humorless breath. \"You’re teasing me. Either way she was just teasing and nothing would have happend anyway.\"","\"Am I wrong?\"","He opened his mouth to answer and found the easy reply missing. He had told himself the whole walk back to the room that Mira’s teasing was harmless, that she saw him as a child playing at adulthood, that responding would only encourage something neither of them meant. Those reasons still existed. They no longer felt complete. Underneath them sat a quieter, uglier hesitation he had not named until the chained figure forced it into the open.","Ekaksha spoke into the gap.","\"You think she will stop looking at you that same way once she knows that what you think of yourself as... Useless.\"","The words were plain. They cut anyway.","Rael’s jaw tightened. The First Realm, the public declaration of classless, the family’s cold dismissal, the way even a stranger’s casual kindness now arrived with an internal flinch—he had carried all of it into the inn without examining the shape it made. Hearing it spoken aloud in this place made the flinch harder to ignore. For him it has become harder to connect with anyone.","For a time he said nothing. The water under him remained almost perfectly still. The seven chains held their eternal tension.","Then, quieter:","\"You’re not useless, Rael. Not to me atleast.\"","The statement came without softness, yet it carried more force than any of the earlier probes. Rael felt something in his chest shift—not healed, not resolved, only acknowledged by a presence that had known him longer than any living relative. He stared at the faceless head and the single pale eye, and the questions he had been holding began to surface in a different order.","\"How do you know about her?\" he asked. \"About Mira. About what I was thinking when she leaned over the table. You told me before that you couldn’t see what I see.\"","Ekaksha did not look away.","\"Have I said that?\"","Rael waited.","\"You and I are one Rael. Remember that.\"","The sentence settled over the water like a change in pressure. Rael had always treated the visits as visitations—something that entered his sleep from elsewhere or rose out of a sealed corner of his own mind. The idea that the boundary was thinner than that, that the figure in the chains shared the same continuous thread of experience, rearranged every previous conversation in retrospect. The help with studies. The quiet presence through childhood. The year of absence after the awakening. All of it sat differently if \"one\" was not a metaphor.","He did not ask for proof. The fact that Ekaksha could speak of Mira’s silver hair and the exact temperature of the embarrassment in the stairwell was already proof enough.","Rael’s next question came out slower, more careful.","\"Was it you?\"","\"Was what me?\"","\"The seal on my Intelligence. The thing that keeps it locked at the bottom of the status screen. Did you put it there?\"","\"No.\"","The answer was immediate and flat.","\"Whoever did that does not mean you well and I only want to see you rise.\"","Rael absorbed the words. An enemy capable of reaching into the foundation of his attributes before he even awakened, of leaving a mark that still resisted every ordinary attempt to understand it. The list of people with that kind of reach was short and dangerous. He filed the knowledge beside the other unfinished debts he carried.","\"What do you want?\" Rael asked at last. \"From me. From this. You’ve been here my whole life. You help when you feel like it, you stay silent when you don’t, and you still won’t say what any of it is for. What do you want?\"","Ekaksha’s formed lips moved in the faintest suggestion of a smile.","\"I already have what I want.\"","The reply gave nothing and closed the subject at the same time. Rael felt the familiar frustration rise and then subside. Some answers still could not be forced. The figure in the chains had never pretended otherwise.","A sound interrupted the stillness—a single, sharp note of metal under strain.","Rael’s attention snapped to the chains. One of them, the heavy link that had circled Ekaksha’s neck, had begun to loosen. The iron looked older than the others, the surface pitted, the tension no longer absolute. As he watched, the final holding strength failed. The chain slipped free of the pale throat and swung outward in a sudden, heavy arc, directly toward him.","Rael threw himself sideways.","He was almost fast enough.","The end of the chain brushed across his open palm as it passed. Pain flared—white, immediate, and wrong in a way ordinary burns were not. He hit the water hard, sending a wide ring of ripples outward, and clutched the hand to his chest. The chain continued its swing and then simply stopped, hanging slack in the air as if the force that had animated it had been spent.","Ekaksha remained suspended by the remaining six chains, the newly freed length of iron dangling uselessly beside him. The single eye watched Rael without visible concern.","\"I can finally move my head.\"","Rael didn’t know what to say this was something that never happened before it was the first time he was seeing any changes in the chains.","\"You should wake up boy,\" the rough voice said.","The water, the sky, and the chained figure began to lose solidity at the edges. Rael tried to speak and found the dream already thinning around the words. The last thing he felt was the continuing burn in his palm, too precise and too deep to belong to ordinary sleep.","He woke in the inn bed with a sharp inhale.","Morning light—Aetherion’s long morning—pressed against the window it was still very dim light. The room was quiet. His body ached in the familiar places from the previous day’s fights. He sat up and opened his right hand.","Across the palm ran a clean, raised scar in the exact pattern of a heavy chain link. The skin around it was pink and tender, the mark already set as if it had been there for days rather than minutes. He closed the hand, opened it again, and felt the phantom heat still lingering under the surface.","Rael stared at the scar for a long time.","\"So it wasn’t just my imagination after all.\"","---"]},{"number":77,"title":"Chapter 77: First Friend In New World","paragraphs":["Rael sat on the edge of the bed with his right hand open under the mana-lamp light. The chain-shaped scar cut cleanly across the palm, raised and still faintly warm, as if the iron had left a memory under the skin. He turned the hand once, then again, and tried to make the pieces fit.","Why had that particular chain come loose? The others had held for as long as he had been visiting that same place for all his life. The neck chain suddenly gone loose did not explain the sudden failure or the way it had swung toward him with purpose. And how could he have seen Ekaksha inside his sleep for an entire life without ever learning what the entity actually was? The questions circled without landing. One conclusion refused to move: no one—human, entity, or otherwise—offered help for years without some form of return. Ekaksha had guided him through childhood lessons, had steadied him in ways Rael still could not fully map, and had asked for nothing obvious in exchange. That absence of visible price made the relationship more confusing, not less. He did not hate the chained figure. He simply no longer trusted the shape of the kindness.","Outside the window the light was thin and blue-gray. Aetherion’s long night was ending; the first of the twin suns had not yet cleared the walls, but the darkness had begun to lift. Early enough that the city still felt half-asleep.","He uncorked one of the minor health potions that he bought from the market before from his storage ring and poured the pale liquid over the scar. The potion sank in. The pain did not change. He waited longer than necessary, then tried a second small application. Nothing. The burn sat outside the reach of ordinary recovery the same way. In the First Realm he had burned his whole arm till black and watched lesser potions close the damage in minutes. This was different—deeper, cleaner, and stubborn. He would need a higher-tier potion or an actual healer. For now he wrapped the palm in clean bandage, tight enough to protect it, loose enough that he could still close his fingers.","Dressing took longer than usual. Every flex of the hand sent a bright, precise spike up his wrist. He gritted his teeth, finished with the straps of his armor, and went downstairs.","The common room was empty. Chairs stood on tables. The hearth held only ash. The air still carried a faint trace of last night’s cooking, but the space itself felt paused. From the kitchen came the soft sounds of someone already working—water, a knife on a board, the low clink of pottery. Footsteps approached the swinging door. Mira pushed through with a cloth over one shoulder and her silver hair tied back more practically than the night before. The bunny ears flicked once when she saw him.","\"Well, well. The serious customer rises with the suns.\" The teasing note was already in her voice. \"Couldn’t sleep, or did you just miss my face?\"","Rael gave a small, tired sound that might have been a laugh. \"Something like that.\"","She started to say more, then her eyes dropped to the white bandage around his right hand. The playful tilt of her head changed.","\"What happened?\"","\"Burned it. Turning off the lamp.\"","Mira’s ears flattened slightly. \"Don’t lie. Our lamps are made of mana-stone. They don’t burn skin.\" She waited. When he offered nothing else she exhaled and nodded once, accepting the silence without pushing. \"All right. Don’t tell me. Sit down anyway. I can help.\"","He sat. She pulled a chair close and held out her own hand, palm up. After a moment he rested his bandaged hand in hers. Her fingers were careful as they unwrapped the cloth. When the chain scar came into view she went still.","\"That’s not a normal burn.\"","\"I noticed.\"","Mira closed her eyes. A soft, cool light gathered around her hands—clean and steady, the unmistakable signature of a healing class. She worked in silence for a time, brows drawn together. The light intensified. Sweat began to show at her temple.","\"You’re a healer,\" Rael said quietly.","\"Among other things.\" She did not look up. \"Don’t worry about payment. I don’t charge for this. The inn already keeps me fed.\"","He watched the stubborn scar slowly lose its angry color under her effort. \"You’re rabbit-folk. In a major human city. And a high-end healer at that. How does that work here?\"","Mira’s mouth quirked, though her focus stayed on the hand. \"Ashfall does a lot of business with mixed-blood communities. Rabbit-folk, fox-kin, a few others. As long as you pay taxes and don’t start trouble, most people only care whether you can do the job. A healer who can close bad wounds is welcome almost anywhere.\" She guided a fresh pulse of light into the deepest part of the mark and added, lighter, \"The ears are just a bonus for the customers.\"","He noticed she had steered the conversation away from the rarity of her class. He did not press.","\"This burn is fighting me,\" she murmured after another stretch of silence. \"Whatever made it wasn’t ordinary fire or acid. I’m having to push harder than I expected.\"","\"You don’t have to—\"","\"I said I’d help.\" The words were firm but not unkind. She poured more of her skill into the work until the last of the raised chain pattern smoothed into ordinary pink skin. When she finally released his hand she sat back and wiped her forehead with the cloth from her shoulder. The cool light faded.","\"There. Try not to collect any more mystery burns for a few days.\"","The teasing tone returned, but it sat thinner than before. Something quieter moved underneath it—tiredness, or an old sadness she had not intended to show. Rael felt the difference the way he had felt the difference between ordinary wolves and the Alpha. He adjusted the way he spoke to match.","\"Thank you. Really.\"","Mira blinked, then smiled with less performance in it. \"You’re welcome, Rael.\"","They sat in the empty common room while the light outside strengthened. Conversation drifted to safer ground. The earlier heat and awkwardness from the previous evening did not return. In its place settled a simpler ease. Rael found himself answering without measuring every word. Mira, for her part, stopped reaching for the sharpest tease and simply talked. He felt like he made a first friend in Second Realm.","When the first real gold of the twin suns crossed the windowsill he stood. She stood with him. On impulse—practical, necessary, and only slightly dishonest—he reached out and closed his hand briefly around hers.","[Class detected]","[Class: Healer (A-Rank)]","[Do you want to copy?]","[Y/N]","The system acknowledgment flickered at the edge of his awareness. He accepted it in the same heartbeat and let go.","Mira looked at their hands, then at his face, ears flicking in clear surprise and with a blush. \"What was that for?\"","\"Thank you for everything Mira.\" Rael said again, and meant it on more than one level.","After sometime he left her in the quiet common room with the morning light and the faint scent of bread beginning to rise from the kitchen, the new class sitting cleanly beside the others, and the chain scar gone as if it had never been more than a dream. Outside, Ashfall was waking. He had cores to sell, a week already paid for, and a long list of questions that still had no answers. For the moment it was enough to walk out of the inn with both hands steady and as for Ekaksha he can’t know anything about him now so he just stopped thinking for now.","---"]}]);
